WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:09.037
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:17.155
Unit twenty-four: The Rhetoric of Advertising. Today you will describe persuasive techniques with 'whose' and 'which' relative clauses.

4
00:00:17.455 --> 00:00:20.038
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.361
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:26.322
Advertising.

7
00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:31.023
Advertising means the business of promoting products or services.

8
00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:33.999
Advertising shapes what we think we need.

9
00:00:34.583 --> 00:00:37.561
Your turn. Say: Advertising.

10
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.598
Advertising.

11
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.281
Persuasion.

12
00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:47.003
Persuasion means the act of convincing someone.

13
00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:50.731
Subtle persuasion is often the most effective.

14
00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.957
Your turn. Say: Persuasion.

15
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.755
Persuasion.

16
00:00:58.517 --> 00:00:59.495
Slogan.

17
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:04.097
Slogan means a short, memorable phrase used in advertising.

18
00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:07.751
The slogan was translated into twelve languages.

19
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.712
Your turn. Say: Slogan.

20
00:01:13.567 --> 00:01:14.661
Slogan.

21
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:16.743
Target audience.

22
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.363
Target audience means the specific group an advert is aimed at.

23
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:25.424
The target audience is students aged 15–20.

24
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:28.890
Your turn. Say: Target audience.

25
00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:33.013
Target audience.

26
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.227
Emotional appeal.

27
00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:40.027
Emotional appeal means a technique that persuades through feelings.

28
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:43.708
The advert relies on emotional appeal rather than facts.

29
00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:46.946
Your turn. Say: Emotional appeal.

30
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.242
Emotional appeal.

31
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:52.729
Brand.

32
00:01:53.717 --> 00:01:56.993
Brand means the identity and image of a company or product.

33
00:01:57.583 --> 00:02:00.070
The brand is associated with youth and freedom.

34
00:02:00.683 --> 00:02:03.347
Your turn. Say: Brand.

35
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.015
Brand.

36
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:08.948
Implicit.

37
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:13.041
Implicit means suggested but not stated directly.

38
00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:16.114
The implicit message is that you will be happier.

39
00:02:16.717 --> 00:02:19.369
Your turn. Say: Implicit.

40
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:23.304
Implicit.

41
00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.179
Consumer.

42
00:02:26.183 --> 00:02:29.437
Consumer means a person who buys goods or services.

43
00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.002
Consumers are increasingly sceptical of adverts.

44
00:02:33.617 --> 00:02:36.223
Your turn. Say: Consumer.

45
00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:40.323
Consumer.

46
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:45.074
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.917 --> 00:02:51.181
Visal, your presentation compared two phone adverts. What made the first one so effective?

48
00:02:52.283 --> 00:03:02.180
It uses a slogan which appeals to emotion rather than logic: 'Never miss a moment'. It sells memories, not technology.

49
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:05.283
And who is the target audience?

50
00:03:06.383 --> 00:03:14.130
Young parents, whose fear of missing their children's childhood is exactly what the advert exploits.

51
00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:17.321
Is the second advert more honest?

52
00:03:18.417 --> 00:03:29.359
In a sense. It lists features, which seems rational, but its implicit message, that serious professionals choose this brand, is still persuasion.

53
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.140
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:32.983 --> 00:03:36.504
Grammar focus: Relative clauses (whose and which).

55
00:03:36.804 --> 00:03:38.025
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:38.717 --> 00:03:41.192
Brands use slogans which appeal to emotion.

57
00:03:42.083 --> 00:03:46.614
Young parents, whose fear of missing out is easily exploited, are the target.

58
00:03:47.517 --> 00:03:49.853
It lists features, which seems rational.

59
00:03:50.800 --> 00:04:02.464
'Whose' shows possession for people, brands and things: 'a brand whose logo is famous'. The noun after 'whose' never takes 'the'. 'Which' can refer to a whole previous clause: '…, which seems rational'.

60
00:04:03.217 --> 00:04:08.284
Be careful! Don't say: Brands, whose slogan are catchy, sell more.

61
00:04:10.233 --> 00:04:13.069
Say: Brands whose slogans are catchy sell more.

62
00:04:13.369 --> 00:04:15.113
Make the noun after 'whose' agree.

63
00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:18.471
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:20.755
Selling More Than Products.

65
00:04:21.055 --> 00:04:24.645
Modern advertising rarely sells a product on its features alone.

66
00:04:25.633 --> 00:04:32.556
Instead, it associates the product with values which consumers already desire, such as freedom, status or belonging.

67
00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:38.887
Advertisers carefully define a target audience whose hopes and anxieties can be addressed directly.

68
00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.767
Much of the persuasion is implicit, which makes it harder for consumers to question.

69
00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:51.261
Media-literate viewers learn to ask not only what is being sold, but what lifestyle is being sold with it.

70
00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:56.264
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:57.117 --> 00:05:00.161
A brand blank logo is famous can charge more.

72
00:05:00.461 --> 00:05:04.149
A, which. B, whose. C, who's.

73
00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.001
The answer is B: whose.

74
00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:12.813
Choose the correct sentence.

75
00:05:13.113 --> 00:05:20.899
A, Parents, whose the children are young, buy it.. B, Parents, whose children are young, buy it.. C, Parents, who their children are young, buy it..

76
00:05:24.433 --> 00:05:28.023
The answer is B: Parents, whose children are young, buy it..

77
00:05:29.183 --> 00:05:32.437
A message that is suggested but not stated is blank.

78
00:05:32.737 --> 00:05:36.483
A, explicit. B, implicit. C, a slogan.

79
00:05:40.033 --> 00:05:42.161
The answer is B: implicit.

80
00:05:43.317 --> 00:05:46.535
The specific group an advert is aimed at is the blank.

81
00:05:46.835 --> 00:05:50.907
A, target audience. B, brand. C, persuasion.

82
00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:56.907
The answer is A: target audience.

83
00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:02.546
According to the reading, what do advertisers associate products with?

84
00:06:02.846 --> 00:06:08.090
A, technical data. B, values consumers desire. C, lower prices.

85
00:06:11.633 --> 00:06:14.678
The answer is B: values consumers desire.

86
00:06:15.867 --> 00:06:18.995
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.115
Which persuasive techniques do advertisers use most?

88
00:06:39.917 --> 00:06:41.394
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:41.694 --> 00:07:17.709
In my view, the technique advertisers use most is emotional appeal, which connects a product with feelings like happiness or belonging. Adverts for soft drinks, for example, rarely mention taste; they show friends laughing together. Celebrity endorsement, whose power comes from our wish to be like stars, is also common. Some argue that consumers are smart enough to see through these strategies. Yet emotional messages influence us even when we recognise them.

90
00:07:18.009 --> 00:07:19.719
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:20.617 --> 00:07:25.579
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and relative clauses (whose and which).

92
00:07:25.879 --> 00:07:30.563
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

93
00:07:30.863 --> 00:07:33.678
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:33.978 --> 00:07:38.349
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

